In unit testing, there are scenarios where you want to **exit your test run early**.

Take this simple test, on the [xUnit](https://xunit.net/) framework:

```c#
public async Task SampleTest()
{
  await Task.Delay(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(15));
  Assert.True(true);
}
```

This is a test with a built-in `15` second **delay** to simulate work.

This runs in a pretty straightforward manner.

![initTestRun](../images/2026/10/initTestRun.png)

Suppose you then have a time **threshold** above which you want a test to **fail** for taking too long.

Typically, you'd do this **within the test**, like so:

```c#
[Fact(Timeout = 10_000)]
public async Task SampleTest()
{
  await Task.Delay(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(15));
  Assert.True(true);
}
```

Here, I am indicating that I want the test to time out after `10` seconds using the `Timeout` attribute, which accepts a delay in **milliseconds**.

The **command-line** test runner returns the following:

![timoutAttribute](../images/2026/10/timoutAttribute.png)

An **IDE test runner** will return something similar:

![IDErunnerTimouut](../images/2026/10/IDErunnerTimouut.png)

### TLDR

**The *xUnit* `Timeout` attribute lets you set a limit for how long a test can run; otherwise, it fails with a timeout.**

